WEBVTT

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:36.360
<v Ira Glass>When they started to prepare for COVID-19 patients at Rogue Regional Medical Center in Southern Oregon, Dr. Somnath Ghosh was part of the team. He's the medical director for the intensive care unit, a pulmonary specialist, and very dedicated to his job. Works long hours, the kind of doctor who gives out his cell phone number to staff, and patients, and families. He tells them to use it anytime. And his wife, Renu, is also a doctor, says he really threw himself into this task.
<v Ira Glass>当俄勒冈州南部的罗格地区医疗中心开始为新冠肺炎患者做准备时，Somnath Ghosh 医生是团队的一员。他是重症监护室的医学主任，也是一名肺科专家，工作非常敬业。他工作时间很长，会把自己的手机号码留给员工、病人和家属。他告诉他们随时可以打给他。他的妻子 Renu 也是一名医生，她说他真的全身心地投入到了这项工作中。

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:43.850
<v Renu Gupta>He spent a lot of his even off time at the hospital trying to gear up for the big wave to hit us.
<v Renu Gupta>他甚至把很多休息时间都花在医院里，为即将到来的大浪潮做准备。

00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:44.470
<v Ira Glass>Right.
<v Ira Glass>没错。

00:00:44.470 --> 00:01:05.410
<v Renu Gupta>And he's a little bit of a geek, so got super excited. He's like, "Oh, we today figured out how to use one ventilator on eight patients instead of-- I think four or six is considered the max standard. So he's been calculating, OK, we'll be able to vent 360 patients, preparing for all of this.
<v Renu Gupta>而且他有点极客精神，所以非常兴奋。他说：“哦，我们今天算出来怎么用一台呼吸机救治八名病人，而不是——我想四到六名被认为是最大标准。所以他一直在计算，好的，我们将能够为 360 名病人提供呼吸支持，为这一切做准备。

00:01:05.410 --> 00:01:32.850
<v Ira Glass>So far, they've been lucky. The pandemic has mostly spared the half million people his hospital serves in Southern Oregon. But I spoke with her husband, Dr. Ghosh, one afternoon last week. He said in total, they've only had three COVID patients that needed intensive care. Dr. Ghosh prepared for a war that he's not having to fight. But all the time, he gets invitations to go join the fight in New York, Louisiana, and other hotspots, begging him to fly out and help.
<v Ira Glass>到目前为止，他们很幸运。疫情在很大程度上绕过了他所在的俄勒冈州南部医院所服务的五十万人。但是上周的一天下午，我和她的丈夫 Ghosh 医生谈了谈。他说，总共只有三名新冠患者需要重症监护。Ghosh 医生为一场他不必参与的战争做了准备。但他一直在收到邀请，要去纽约、路易斯安那州和其他热点地区加入战斗，请求他飞过去帮忙。

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:41.760
<v Somnath Ghosh>Every single day. In fact, I get at least four or five of these a day. And I've been getting them for the last few weeks. I can read you a few.
<v Somnath Ghosh>每天都如此。事实上，我每天至少会收到四到五个这样的请求。过去几周我一直都在收到它们。我可以给你读几条。

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:42.350
<v Ira Glass>Sure.
<v Ira Glass>当然。

00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:54.750
<v Somnath Ghosh>"Hi, Dr. Ghosh. It's Elizabeth with Core Medical. I hope you're doing well. We have a client in New York who needs critical care support. Due to a high volume of patients, I hope to connect with you soon. Thank you, for all you do."
<v Somnath Ghosh>“你好，Ghosh 医生。我是 Core Medical 的 Elizabeth。希望你一切都好。我们在纽约有一位客户需要重症监护支持。由于病人数量庞大，希望尽快与你联系。感谢你所做的一切。”

00:01:54.750 --> 00:02:11.980
<v Somnath Ghosh>Another one says, "Immediate ICU coverage needed. Should you be able to assist, please give me a call or send a CV to" yadda, yadda, yadda. "We can license." Another one says, "Good morning. A facility in Houston area is needing some help in their ICU. Can you help them during this crisis?"
<v Somnath Ghosh>另一条说：“急需 ICU 人手。如果你能提供帮助，请给我打电话或将简历发送到……”云云。我们可以提供执照。”另一条说：“早上好。休斯顿地区的一家医疗机构需要 ICU 方面的帮助。在这个危机时刻，你能帮帮他们吗？”

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:19.710
<v Somnath Ghosh>I also have received texts from friends, personally. They're in such a need for critical care physicians.
<v Somnath Ghosh>我还收到了朋友们的个人短信。他们非常需要重症监护医师。

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:21.530
<v Ira Glass>Do you wish you can go?
<v Ira Glass>你希望你能去吗？

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:44.810
<v Somnath Ghosh>Oh, hell yeah. Hell, yeah. I mean, this-- I mean, this is what I trained for. This is what I do. This is six years of specialty training, and then nine years of experience. I feel useless sitting here in Southern Oregon while the Northeast is getting pummeled.
<v Somnath Ghosh>哦，天哪，当然想。我是说，这——这是我受过训练要去做的事情。这就是我的工作。这是六年专业培训和九年的经验。我坐在俄勒冈州南部，而东北部正遭受重创，我感到自己毫无用处。

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:49.890
<v Ira Glass>Yeah. Like there's a historic medical emergency that could use your exact special skills.
<v Ira Glass>是的。就像有一场历史性的医疗紧急情况，正需要你这种专门的技能。

00:02:49.890 --> 00:03:07.520
<v Somnath Ghosh>Yeah. Yeah. I mean, I feel guilty even going home and playing with my kids and watching TV. And I know my friends and my brethren on the East Coast are risking their lives, suboptimal PPE, not enough specialists to help them.
<v Somnath Ghosh>是啊。我是说，我甚至为回家陪孩子玩、看电视而感到内疚。我知道我在东海岸的朋友和同僚们正冒着生命危险，在个人防护装备不足、缺乏专家帮助的情况下工作。

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:19.260
<v Renu Gupta>So he comes back from work one day. I'm in the midst of getting dinner going for our two children, and he's like, what do you think about me going to New York? I am sitting here. Nothing is happening. And there, they are just dying.
<v Renu Gupta>所以有一天他下班回来。我正忙着给我们两个孩子准备晚餐，他说，我去纽约怎么样？我坐在这里。什么事都没发生。而在那里，他们正在死去。

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:51.220
<v Renu Gupta>And I think my first flash action was just anger. I'm like, how can you be so selfish? You only always think as a physician. And me, or the kids, or family, just you don't even think about it. And trying to go to New York? Especially, looking for trouble? I'm like, there are other doctors, but I only have one husband. My kids have only one dad. And you're the only son to your mom, who's a widow now and kind of depends on you for everything.
<v Renu Gupta>我想我的第一反应只是愤怒。我在想，你怎么能这么自私？你总是只以医生的身份思考。而我，或者孩子们，或者家庭，你甚至都不考虑。还想去纽约？特别是，去自找麻烦？我想，还有其他医生，但我只有一个丈夫。我的孩子们只有一个爸爸。而你是你母亲唯一的儿子，她现在是个寡妇，几乎在各方面都依赖你。

00:03:51.220 --> 00:04:20.120
<v Renu Gupta>But then I think 10 minutes later, I was telling myself that I'm not surprised by this. He's at his best when he's the physician. He works harder than anything he'll work at at home. He's more patient with his staff and patients than he is with my children. It's just how he connects. I think there's some part of him which just gets very satisfied when he's able to help someone.
<v Renu Gupta>但十分钟后，我告诉自己，我对此并不感到惊讶。当他作为一名医生时，他处于最佳状态。他工作起来比在家里做的任何事情都要努力。他对员工和病人比对我的孩子更有耐心。这就是他建立联系的方式。我想他内心深处有一部分，当他能够帮助别人时，会感到非常满足。

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:45.510
<v Ira Glass>But he's not going to go to New York, and not because of her. Not because of their kids, who are still little. Not because he's worried he'll get COVID. He's young and healthy and has a doctor's overconfidence, assuming he's going to be fine. He's not going to save lives like he wants, because he can't go. And he can't go because of his immigration status. He's in the United States on something called an H1B visa.
<v Ira Glass>但他不会去纽约，不是因为她。不是因为他们还在年幼的孩子。不是因为他担心会感染新冠。他年轻健康，有着医生那种过度自信，以为自己会没事的。他无法像他想的那样去拯救生命，因为他不能去。而他不能去是因为他的移民身份。他在美国持有一种叫做 H1B 的签证。

00:04:45.510 --> 00:04:51.950
<v Somnath Ghosh>And the rules prohibit me from working for anyone else other than my employer.
<v Somnath Ghosh>而且规定禁止我为雇主以外的任何人工作。

00:04:51.950 --> 00:04:59.090
<v Ira Glass>So he has to stay in Oregon. He can't go to work for a hospital in Brooklyn or New Orleans. He can't even volunteer.
<v Ira Glass>所以他必须留在俄勒冈州。他不能去布鲁克林或新奥尔良的医院工作。他甚至不能做志愿者。

00:04:59.090 --> 00:05:15.550
<v Ira Glass>We're in a health emergency in the United States. And over a fourth of the physicians here are foreign born. Over 8,000 of those have the kind of visa that Somnath has, an H1B visa, which is given to people who have special education or skills and want to work in the United States.
<v Ira Glass>美国正处于卫生紧急状态。而这里超过四分之一的医生是外国出生的。其中超过 8,000 人拥有像 Somnath 那样的签证，即 H1B 签证，这是发给那些拥有特殊教育或技能并希望在美国工作的人的。

00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:34.380
<v Ira Glass>In Somnath's case, he was born in India, came here for his medical residency, got his H1B visa when he got his first job out of training at the hospital where he still works nine years later. If he had a green card, it would fix the problem. And he's waiting for one, but the backlog on green cards is massive. That'll take decades.
<v Ira Glass>就 Somnath 而言，他出生在印度，来这里完成了医学住院医师实习，在九年前从培训毕业后的第一份工作时就拿到了 H1B 签证，至今仍在同一家医院工作。如果他有绿卡，问题就解决了。他正在等待绿卡，但绿卡的积压非常严重。那需要几十年。

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:54.990
<v Ira Glass>He believes the government should grant exceptions to the rules right now and let health workers with H1B visas fly to the hotspots and help out during this crisis. And dozens of senators and Congress people, mostly Democrats, but a few Republicans sprinkled in, have tried to get the US Citizenship and Immigration Service to do just that, though that hasn't gotten much traction.
<v Ira Glass>他认为政府现在应该对规则给予豁免，让持有 H1B 签证的医护人员飞往热点地区，在危机期间提供帮助。数十名参议员和国会议员，主要是民主党人，也有少数共和党人，试图让美国公民及移民服务局这样做，但这并没有得到太多的推动。

00:05:54.990 --> 00:06:00.410
<v Ira Glass>So Dr. Ghosh is stuck in Southern Oregon during this crisis, far from the action.
<v Ira Glass>所以 Ghosh 医生在这次危机期间被困在俄勒冈州南部，远离一线。

00:06:00.410 --> 00:06:03.590
<v Ira Glass>What if you just went? Couldn't you just go?
<v Ira Glass>如果你直接过去会怎么样？你就不能直接去吗？

00:06:03.590 --> 00:06:09.470
<v Somnath Ghosh>Well, if I do that, then I violate the terms of my H1B, and it becomes null and void.
<v Somnath Ghosh>嗯，如果我那样做，我就违反了 H1B 的条款，它将变得无效。

00:06:09.470 --> 00:06:13.340
<v Ira Glass>Oh, I see. Then you'd be here illegally, all of a sudden.
<v Ira Glass>哦，我明白了。那么你突然就会成为非法滞留了。

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:20.180
<v Somnath Ghosh>Well, yeah. Then I'll be out of status, which means I'll be unemployed. And yeah, that thought did cross my mind. Trust me.
<v Somnath Ghosh>嗯，是的。那么我就失去了身份，这意味着我将失业。是的，那个想法确实闪过我的脑海。相信我。

00:06:20.180 --> 00:06:22.520
<v Ira Glass>But the cost is just too high.
<v Ira Glass>但代价太高了。

00:06:22.520 --> 00:06:41.150
<v Somnath Ghosh>Yeah. If I was just by myself, sure. I'd do what I thought was right, and then I could just up and leave, go back to India or elsewhere. With a wife and a 3-year-old and a six-year-old, yeah, I can't risk that.
<v Somnath Ghosh>是的。如果只是我一个人，当然。我会做我认为正确的事，然后我可以直接起身离开，回印度或其他地方。有了妻子和一个 3 岁、一个 6 岁的孩子，是的，我冒不起这个险。

00:06:41.150 --> 00:06:50.450
<v Ira Glass>Yeah. How does it make you feel that you can't help?
<v Ira Glass>是的。无法提供帮助让你有什么感觉？

00:06:50.450 --> 00:07:14.890
<v Somnath Ghosh>Frustrated. Small. I mean, you'd think that common sense will prevail and they'd do what's right for the population, especially in such trying times. You want to yell and shout and scream and say, what exactly are you guys thinking of?
<v Somnath Ghosh>沮丧。渺小。我的意思是，你本以为常识会占上风，他们会为民众做正确的事，尤其是在这种困难时期。你想大喊大叫，质问他们到底在想什么？

00:07:14.890 --> 00:07:27.620
<v Ira Glass>Dr. Ghosh says before this, he felt happy in Southern Oregon. He's one of the leaders in his hospital. He felt like he belonged. But since COVID, for the first time he's started to think he might leave the United States.
<v Ira Glass>Ghosh 医生说在此之前，他在俄勒冈州南部感到很幸福。他是医院的领导之一。他觉得他属于这里。但自从新冠疫情以来，他第一次开始考虑离开美国。

00:07:27.620 --> 00:07:35.530
<v Somnath Ghosh>I've been thinking about this more and more, because my kids are only 3 and 6. They can easily adapt to a new way of life.
<v Somnath Ghosh>我一直在思考这个问题，因为我的孩子才 3 岁和 6 岁。他们可以很容易地适应新的生活方式。

00:07:35.530 --> 00:07:35.870
<v Ira Glass>Mm-hm.
<v Ira Glass>嗯。

00:07:35.870 --> 00:08:06.650
<v Somnath Ghosh>I'll be honest. The community in Southern Oregon has embraced me, and this is home for me. I was happy just doing my job, raising my family. And then when this thing hit and I realized how helpless I am and how inconsequential, it boggles my mind that they don't think that this is a priority. If this happens again this winter with an outbreak and this repeats itself, I'm out of here. I'm done.
<v Somnath Ghosh>老实说。俄勒冈州南部的社区接纳了我，这里是我的家。我很高兴能做好我的工作，抚养我的家庭。然后当这件事发生时，我意识到自己是多么无助和无关紧要，他们不认为这是优先事项，这让我感到震惊。如果今年冬天再发生一次爆发，这种情况再次重演，我就离开这里。我受够了。

00:08:06.650 --> 00:08:08.180
<v Ira Glass>What do you mean?
<v Ira Glass>你是什么意思？

00:08:08.180 --> 00:08:32.710
<v Somnath Ghosh>Oh, like if this winter season there's another surge and then we're hit with the flu, and the coronavirus, and a personnel and equipment shortage, and they need help, I'm not going to sit around just going through this rigmarole all over again. I'm going to pack my bags and leave. Maybe make a stop in New York or something for a few weeks before I leave.
<v Somnath Ghosh>哦，比如如果这个冬季又有一次浪潮，然后我们同时受到流感和冠状病毒的冲击，再加上人员和设备短缺，他们需要帮助，我不会坐视这一切再次发生。我会收拾行李离开。也许在离开前在纽约停留几周什么的。

00:08:32.710 --> 00:08:42.740
<v Ira Glass>So the plan would be to leave, take the whole family back to India. And maybe on the way, you'd work for a few weeks or months in a New York hospital, since at that point it wouldn't matter.
<v Ira Glass>所以计划是离开，把全家带回印度。也许在路上，你会去纽约的一家医院工作几周或几个月，因为到那时这已经无所谓了。

00:08:42.740 --> 00:08:49.900
<v Somnath Ghosh>Might as well. I mean, if I'm going to get out of here, I might as well do some good along the way.
<v Somnath Ghosh>最好还是去吧。我的意思是，如果我要离开这里，我不如在路上做点好事。

00:08:49.900 --> 00:09:07.330
<v Ira Glass>When I asked his wife, Renu, about that plan, she was like, well, we'll decide about that together. For now anyway, Somnath is stuck, getting texts like this one that he got recently from a friend, a guy who he went to medical school with, who's now in New Jersey, deluged with COVID cases.
<v Ira Glass>当我问他的妻子 Renu 关于那个计划时，她说，好吧，我们会一起决定这件事。无论如何，现在 Somnath 被困住了，收到像他最近从一位朋友那里收到的这样的短信，那是他医学院的同学，现在在新泽西州，被新冠病例淹没了。

00:09:07.330 --> 00:09:20.950
<v Somnath Ghosh>So here he says, "Hey, dude, haven't heard back from you in a few days. What happened to your plans of coming here? We could certainly use you. Getting our asses kicked here. Besides, I haven't seen you forever."
<v Somnath Ghosh>这里他写道：“嘿，伙计，几天没听到你的回信了。你来这里的计划怎么了？我们肯定用得着你。我们在这里被搞得团团转。再说，我已经很久没见到你了。”

00:09:20.950 --> 00:09:35.750
<v Somnath Ghosh>So I reply, "So apparently cannot help, due to H1B visa." So he replies, "Are you f-ing kidding me? We are using residents from unrelated specialties in our ICU. You're just going to sit there?"
<v Somnath Ghosh>所以我回复：“看来因为 H1B 签证，无法提供帮助。”他回复说：“你在开什么国际玩笑？我们都在 ICU 里使用来自非相关专业的住院医师了。你就打算坐在那里吗？”

00:09:35.750 --> 00:09:43.570
<v Somnath Ghosh>So I reply, "What do you want me to do?" And he's like, "This is bullshit, bro. I gotta go. Talk to you later."
<v Somnath Ghosh>所以我回复：“你想让我怎么做？”他说：“这真是胡扯，兄弟。我得走了。回头聊。”

00:09:43.570 --> 00:09:58.340
<v Ira Glass>Later in our program, Stuck. There's so many of us stuck at home, or stuck without a job, or stuck on hold for everything next that was going to happen in our lives, waiting for a future to arrive that now seems unreadable and impenetrable.
<v Ira Glass>节目稍后，困境。我们中有这么多人被困在家里，或被困在没有工作的地方，或被困在等待生活中发生的下一件事，等待着一个现在看起来难以解读和难以逾越的未来。

00:09:58.340 --> 00:10:21.520
<v Ira Glass>We have stories of people stuck in some unusual and extreme situations with-- I swear, we are human beings here at our show, we need this, too-- with some happy endings in some stories. From WBEZ Chicago, this is This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>我们有一些人被困在不同寻常和极端情况下的故事——我发誓，我们节目里的人也是有血有肉的，我们也需要这个——有些故事有圆满的结局。来自芝加哥 WBEZ，这里是《美国生活》。我是 Ira Glass。请继续收听。

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:36.640
<v Ira Glass>Act One, You Can Check Out Anytime You Like, But You Can Never Leave. So our show today is about people who are stuck. And years ago, one of our co-workers, Elna Baker, found herself trapped in a hotel room with her siblings, held there by a stranger.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，你可以随时结账，但你永远无法离开。所以我们今天的节目是关于被困住的人。多年前，我们的同事 Elna Baker 发现自己和兄弟姐妹被困在酒店房间里，被一个陌生人挟持。

00:10:36.640 --> 00:10:59.640
<v Ira Glass>What happened was, their family was all staying at this Marriott. But they're such a big family, four of the kids were in one room, with the oldest kid and the parents next door. Elna was 14 years old at the time, in the room with 10-year-old Julia, 8-year-old Britain, and 5-year-old Jill, who Elna was sharing a bed with. Elna, the 14-year-old, the eighth grader, was more or less in charge.
<v Ira Glass>事情是这样的，他们的家人都住在万豪酒店。但他们是一个大家庭，四个孩子住一间房，大一点的孩子和父母住在隔壁。Elna 当时 14 岁，和 10 岁的 Julia、8 岁的 Britain 以及 5 岁的 Jill 住在同一个房间，Elna 和 Jill 同床。14 岁的 Elna，八年级的学生，或多或少负责照顾她们。

00:10:59.640 --> 00:11:02.640
<v Ira Glass>And then one night they're all asleep, it's the middle of the night.
<v Ira Glass>有一天晚上他们都睡着了，正是午夜。

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:15.240
<v Elna Baker>My memory is that it's like I saw like a sliver of light, which is what woke me up, and the sound of the door closing. And there was the feeling that there was someone standing at the foot of the bed.
<v Elna Baker>我的记忆是，我看到了像一丝光亮，那是把我惊醒的东西，还有门关上的声音。有一种感觉，床尾站着一个人。

00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:16.100
<v Ira Glass>Mm-hm.
<v Ira Glass>嗯。

00:11:16.100 --> 00:11:21.680
<v Elna Baker>So I reached over, and I turned the lamp on. And there was this woman.
<v Elna Baker>所以我伸手打开了灯。有一个女人在那里。

00:11:21.680 --> 00:11:22.160
<v Ira Glass>Uh-huh.
<v Ira Glass>嗯哼。

00:11:22.160 --> 00:11:35.950
<v Elna Baker>And she looked startled to see us. And the first thing she said was, you're just children. I didn't expect you to be children.
<v Elna Baker>她看到我们时显得很吃惊。她说的第一句话是，你们只是孩子。我没想到你们会是孩子。

00:11:35.950 --> 00:11:37.390
<v Ira Glass>So that's pretty creepy.
<v Ira Glass>这挺令人毛骨悚然的。

00:11:37.390 --> 00:11:38.110
<v Elna Baker>Right.
<v Elna Baker>对。

00:11:38.110 --> 00:11:40.870
<v Ira Glass>What did you make of her saying that?
<v Ira Glass>你认为她那么说是什么意思？

00:11:40.870 --> 00:11:48.360
<v Elna Baker>I instantly felt like this is an unsafe situation.
<v Elna Baker>我立刻感觉到这是一种不安全的情况。

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:51.820
<v Ira Glass>Yeah. Describe her.
<v Ira Glass>是啊。描述一下她。

00:11:51.820 --> 00:11:55.600
<v Elna Baker>I remember she was my mom's age, so she was probably in her 40s.
<v Elna Baker>我记得她和我妈妈年纪差不多，所以她可能 40 多岁。

00:11:55.600 --> 00:11:56.710
<v Ira Glass>Mm-hm.
<v Ira Glass>嗯哼。

00:11:56.710 --> 00:12:06.850
<v Elna Baker>And brown hair, white, frumpy looking, like somebody you'd sit next to on the bus or something.
<v Elna Baker>棕色头发，白人，看起来邋邋遢遢的，就像你在公交车上旁边会坐的那种人。

00:12:06.850 --> 00:12:09.310
<v Ira Glass>Did she seem menacing?
<v Ira Glass>她看起来有威胁性吗？

00:12:09.310 --> 00:12:15.280
<v Elna Baker>She seemed really distressed. She was crying, too.
<v Elna Baker>她看起来真的很痛苦。她也在哭。

00:12:15.280 --> 00:12:16.120
<v Ira Glass>She was crying?
<v Ira Glass>她在哭？

00:12:16.120 --> 00:12:19.790
<v Elna Baker>She was crying, yep. She cried the whole time she was there.
<v Elna Baker>她在哭，是的。她在那里的时候一直在哭。

00:12:19.790 --> 00:12:24.250
<v Ira Glass>She's crying, and she said, "You're children. I didn't expect you to be children."
<v Ira Glass>她在哭，她说：“你们是孩子。我没想到你们会是孩子。”

00:12:24.250 --> 00:12:26.320
<v Elna Baker>Yeah.
<v Elna Baker>是啊。

00:12:26.320 --> 00:12:28.600
<v Ira Glass>And did the other kids get up?
<v Ira Glass>其他孩子起来了吗？

00:12:28.600 --> 00:12:39.710
<v Elna Baker>Everyone's wide awake. I distinctly remember my little brothers' and sisters faces, because their eyes were just huge. But no one said anything.
<v Elna Baker>所有人都醒着。我清楚地记得我弟弟妹妹们的脸，因为他们的眼睛睁得大大的。但没人说话。

00:12:39.710 --> 00:12:42.610
<v Ira Glass>And are they sitting up, too? Or are they just laying flat still?
<v Ira Glass>他们也坐起来了吗？还是只是平躺着不动？

00:12:42.610 --> 00:12:53.030
<v Elna Baker>They're still laying flat. Like, nobody moved.
<v Elna Baker>他们仍然平躺着。就像，没人动。

00:12:53.030 --> 00:12:54.680
<v Ira Glass>So what happens next?
<v Ira Glass>那么接下来发生了什么？

00:12:54.680 --> 00:13:08.420
<v Elna Baker>She's talking, right? This isn't how it was supposed to be. And she's also crying. And she's talking to herself. I remember it being very hard to follow what she was saying.
<v Elna Baker>她在说话，对吧？事情本不该是这样的。她也在哭。她在自言自语。我记得很难听懂她在说什么。

00:13:08.420 --> 00:13:09.370
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是啊。

00:13:09.370 --> 00:13:35.090
<v Elna Baker>And eventually I said, I'm sorry. I think you have the wrong room. And she started to cry, and she said, I know, I know. I do this, I do this. I don't know why I do this. And then she's pacing back and forth and crying. And she sits down on the bed, at the foot of the bed.
<v Elna Baker>最终我说，对不起。我想你走错房间了。她开始哭，她说，我知道，我知道。我做了这种事，我做了这事。我不知道我为什么要这么做。然后她在来回踱步和哭泣。她在床尾坐了下来。

00:13:35.090 --> 00:13:38.750
<v Ira Glass>Did she seem crazy? Or did she seem upset?
<v Ira Glass>她看起来疯了吗？还是看起来很心烦？

00:13:38.750 --> 00:13:46.750
<v Elna Baker>I mean, it's interesting. I think I-- I was too young and unfamiliar to identify what a crazy person seems like.
<v Elna Baker>嗯，这很有趣。我想我——我当时太年轻，不熟悉疯子看起来是什么样的。

00:13:46.750 --> 00:13:47.120
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是啊。

00:13:47.120 --> 00:13:53.250
<v Elna Baker>But what I could identify in that moment was that this person was unpredictable.
<v Elna Baker>但我能在那一刻识别出的是，这个人是不可预测的。

00:13:53.250 --> 00:13:56.310
<v Ira Glass>So you were just like, oh, I've got to be really careful how I do this.
<v Ira Glass>所以你只是想，哦，我得非常小心我怎么做。

00:13:56.310 --> 00:14:07.620
<v Elna Baker>I remember feeling that pressure and, in that moment, very, very, very aware of my brothers and sisters, because they were so little.
<v Elna Baker>我记得感到那种压力，那一刻，非常非常非常关注我的弟弟妹妹们，因为他们太小了。

00:14:07.620 --> 00:14:07.880
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是啊。

00:14:07.880 --> 00:14:33.770
<v Elna Baker>And that something bad could happen to them. And that the way that it wasn't going to happen to them was me. There were no adults there. I think it was the first situation I was ever in where there was-- you couldn't defer to someone else to keep you safe or tell you what to do. And what do I do?
<v Elna Baker>而且他们可能会发生坏事。而防止他们发生那种事的方法就是我。那里没有成年人。我认为这是我曾经经历过的第一个情况，在那里没有——你不能指望别人来保证你的安全或告诉你该怎么做。我该怎么办？

00:14:33.770 --> 00:15:01.380
<v Elna Baker>And it had that quality of like, time slows down. And you're there, but you're also watching. And so then I did the math in my head, and I was like, OK, I have a couple options. They are cry for help, scream, but I don't think anyone will hear.
<v Elna Baker>那种感觉就像时间变慢了。你身处其中，但你也在观察。所以在心里做了一个计算，我想，好的，我有几个选择。它们是呼救、尖叫，但我认为没人会听到。

00:15:01.380 --> 00:15:04.220
<v Ira Glass>Mm-hm.
<v Ira Glass>嗯哼。

00:15:04.220 --> 00:15:19.340
<v Elna Baker>I remember looking at how far away the phone was from me, and I realized I can reach and grab the phone, and I could call for help. Or even like if I could knock on the door where my sister was--
<v Elna Baker>我记得看了看电话离我有多远，我意识到我可以伸手去拿电话，我可以求救。或者即使我可以敲响我姐妹隔壁连接门的门——

00:15:19.340 --> 00:15:21.830
<v Ira Glass>Sister in the connecting room next door.
<v Ira Glass>姐妹在隔壁连接房间。

00:15:21.830 --> 00:15:26.410
<v Elna Baker>I could-- at least they would know something was happening in here, and they could get to us in time.
<v Elna Baker>我可以——至少他们会知道这里发生了一些事，他们能及时赶来帮我们。

00:15:26.410 --> 00:15:27.440
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是啊。

00:15:27.440 --> 00:15:53.840
<v Elna Baker>But all of that felt like an escalation, like I would provoke her. And so what can I do to not provoke her and play up the fact that we're children? And what I felt I needed to do was I needed to calm her down and become her friend. And if I could become her friend, she would leave, and she wouldn't hurt us.
<v Elna Baker>但所有这些都感觉像是升级，好像会激怒她。所以为了不激怒她，发挥我们是孩子这一事实，我能做什么呢？我觉得我需要做的是让她平静下来，成为她的朋友。如果我能成为她的朋友，她就会离开，她就不会伤害我们。

00:15:53.840 --> 00:15:56.840
<v Ira Glass>Like, get her to see you as a person.
<v Ira Glass>就像，让她把你当成一个人看。

00:15:56.840 --> 00:16:26.020
<v Elna Baker>It was like, make her think I was on her side. Because then it didn't feel like she didn't see us as people. It just felt dangerous, and I didn't understand why. And I felt like the key to being safe was slowing everything down.
<v Elna Baker>就像是，让她以为我站在她这一边。因为那样就不觉得她没把我们当人看。只是觉得很危险，我不明白为什么。我觉得安全的关键是让一切慢下来。

00:16:26.020 --> 00:16:31.120
<v Elna Baker>And I remember her saying, I'm really sorry. I'm sorry. I'm sorry I'm here. I'm sorry I'm doing this. I'm sorry.
<v Elna Baker>我记得她说，我真的很抱歉。对不起。我很抱歉我在这里。对不起我做了这件事。对不起。

00:16:31.120 --> 00:16:31.930
<v Ira Glass>Mm-hm.
<v Ira Glass>嗯哼。

00:16:31.930 --> 00:16:43.820
<v Elna Baker>And I don't remember this, but my brother remembers that what she said was, I'm sorry, girls. I'm sorry, girls. And that his whole memory of this night was just being like, I'm a boy.
<v Elna Baker>我不记得了，但我哥哥记得她说的是，对不起，女孩们。对不起，女孩们。他那天晚上的全部记忆就是像，我是个男孩。

00:16:43.820 --> 00:16:45.490
<v Elna Baker>[LAUGHTER]
<v Elna Baker>[笑声]

00:16:45.490 --> 00:16:48.560
<v Elna Baker>He was so mad.
<v Elna Baker>他当时非常生气。

00:16:48.560 --> 00:17:02.980
<v Elna Baker>And so I ask her. I said, are you OK? Is everything OK? And she kept going on about how her night was so hard. And I was like, I'm so sorry. That sounds really, really difficult.
<v Elna Baker>所以我问她。我说，你还好吗？一切都还好吗？她一直喋喋不休地说她的夜晚有多么困难。我说，我很抱歉。听起来真的很艰难。

00:17:02.980 --> 00:17:04.600
<v Ira Glass>And this is you trying to befriend her?
<v Ira Glass>这是你试图和她交朋友？

00:17:04.600 --> 00:17:16.830
<v Elna Baker>Yeah, this is me trying to befriend her. But it's weird. I feel like I was hyper alert to, if I can understand what's going on with her, I can help solve it. But I couldn't understand, because it didn't make any sense.
<v Elna Baker>是的，这是我试图和她交朋友。但这很奇怪。我觉得我高度警觉，如果我能理解她身上发生了什么，我就能帮忙解决。但我无法理解，因为它一点也不合理。

00:17:16.830 --> 00:17:18.780
<v Ira Glass>Did you feel sorry for her?
<v Ira Glass>你为她感到难过吗？

00:17:18.780 --> 00:17:21.660
<v Elna Baker>I felt concerned for her.
<v Elna Baker>我为她感到担忧。

00:17:21.660 --> 00:17:22.970
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是啊。

00:17:22.970 --> 00:17:34.940
<v Elna Baker>And then she took out her purse. And she reached into her purse, and then she gripped onto something. And I remember thinking like, oh, no. The way-- I thought--
<v Elna Baker>然后她拿出了她的钱包。她伸手进钱包，抓住了什么东西。我记得我想，哦，不。那种方式——我想——

00:17:34.940 --> 00:17:36.380
<v Ira Glass>Oh, like it's like a gun or something?
<v Ira Glass>哦，就像枪什么的？

00:17:36.380 --> 00:17:47.160
<v Elna Baker>Or a gun, or a knife, or something. I remember my heart just dropping. And then she pulled out a hairbrush. And then she just started brushing her hair, like at the foot of my bed.
<v Elna Baker>或者是枪，或者是刀，或者其他什么。我记得我的心沉了下去。然后她掏出了一把梳子。然后她就开始梳头，就在我床尾。

00:17:47.160 --> 00:17:49.010
<v Ira Glass>Wow.
<v Ira Glass>哇。

00:17:49.010 --> 00:17:54.600
<v Elna Baker>And then at that point, I was like, this is really weird. Like this is like--
<v Elna Baker>在那一刻，我就想，这真的很奇怪。这就像是——

00:17:54.600 --> 00:17:56.300
<v Ira Glass>Right.
<v Ira Glass>对。

00:17:56.300 --> 00:18:16.070
<v Elna Baker>And I don't think she's going to leave. And then she sat on the vanity in front of the mirror at the desk, and she took her earrings off.
<v Elna Baker>我不认为她会离开。然后她在书桌前的梳妆台上坐下，取下了耳环。

00:18:16.070 --> 00:18:17.000
<v Ira Glass>Oh, wow.
<v Ira Glass>哦，哇。

00:18:17.000 --> 00:18:22.600
<v Elna Baker>And she started taking-- she took her shoes off. She took her coat off. She was just like--
<v Elna Baker>然后她开始脱——她脱掉了鞋子。她脱掉了外套。她就像是——

00:18:22.600 --> 00:18:23.590
<v Ira Glass>Oh, wow.
<v Ira Glass>哦，哇。

00:18:23.590 --> 00:18:27.670
<v Elna Baker>--making herself comfortable to be here now.
<v Elna Baker>——让她自己现在舒服地待在这里。

00:18:27.670 --> 00:18:28.400
<v Ira Glass>Right.
<v Ira Glass>对。

00:18:28.400 --> 00:18:55.280
<v Elna Baker>And so I let her do all that and was listening to her go on about what was wrong. And then, eventually, after I felt like enough time had passed, I said we have to wake up early to go to school tomorrow. And we're really tired, so I think we should probably go back to bed now.
<v Elna Baker>所以我让她做了所有这些，听她讲什么地方出错了。然后，最终，在我觉得时间足够久了之后，我说我们要早起去上学。我们真的很累，所以我想我们现在应该上床睡觉了。

00:18:55.280 --> 00:19:11.510
<v Elna Baker>And she was like, I know, I know. I'm sorry, I'm sorry, I'm sorry I'm here. I'm here. I'm sorry I'm here. And I said, no, it's totally fine that you're here-- almost like thank you for being here-- but do you mind leaving now?
<v Elna Baker>她说，我知道，我知道。对不起，对不起，对不起我在这里。我在这里。对不起我在这里。我说，不，你在这里完全没关系——几乎就像谢谢你在这里——但你介意现在离开吗？

00:19:11.510 --> 00:19:33.790
<v Elna Baker>And so I very slowly stood up and walked over to the door. And every moment was making sure we're safe. And then I opened the door. And she said, OK, OK. And put her shoes on. And then she started to leave. And just as she was about to leave, I said, oh, you forgot your earrings.
<v Elna Baker>所以我非常缓慢地站起来，走到门口。每一个时刻都是为了确保我们安全。然后我打开了门。她说，好的，好的。并穿上了鞋子。然后她开始离开。就在她要离开的时候，我说，哦，你忘了耳环了。

00:19:33.790 --> 00:19:49.930
<v Elna Baker>And then she started crying all over again, like it was this huge thing. Walked over to get the earrings, and then sat back down on the bed. And I remember thinking, god damn. Why did I bring up the earrings? We were so close to being safe.
<v Elna Baker>然后她又开始哭了起来，好像那是什么天大的事。走回来拿耳环，然后又在床上坐下。我记得想，该死。我为什么要提耳环的事？我们离安全只有一步之遥。

00:19:49.930 --> 00:20:24.380
<v Elna Baker>So then basically, the whole thing started all over again where I had to listen to her cry, calm her down. And then finally, I'm like, you know, we have to wake up early to go to school. Do you mind leaving? And I walked her to the door, and she walked out of the door. And as soon as she was out of the door, I just pushed the door as hard as I could to close it. And when I pushed it, basically like slammed it shut, she screamed at the top of her lungs.
<v Elna Baker>所以基本上，整件事又重新开始，我不得不听她哭，让她平静下来。最后，我说，你知道，我们要早起去上学。你介意离开吗？我把她带到门口，她走了出去。她一出门，我就拼尽全力推门关上。当我推门的时候，基本上就像重重地关上了，她放声尖叫。

00:20:24.380 --> 00:20:26.560
<v Ira Glass>And is there a bolt or something on the door?
<v Ira Glass>门上有插销吗？

00:20:26.560 --> 00:20:37.330
<v Elna Baker>I think I did bolt it, after I slammed it shut. And then I heard her screaming in the hallway. And then she ran down the hallway screaming. And then she ran back towards the door screaming.
<v Elna Baker>我想在我用力关上它之后，我确实上了插销。然后我听到她在走廊里尖叫。然后她尖叫着跑下走廊。然后她又尖叫着跑回门边。

00:20:37.330 --> 00:20:38.540
<v Ira Glass>That must've been terrifying.
<v Ira Glass>那一定很可怕。

00:20:38.540 --> 00:21:09.260
<v Elna Baker>It was so scary. And then it was quiet. And then I heard this loud-- she's like banging on the door, as hard as she can, right? And then I hear a voice, and it's my dad. And my dad's like, open the door, open the door! And I realize it's not her banging on the door. So I open the door, and my dad's standing there. And he's like, did you hear someone screaming in the hallway?
<v Elna Baker>那太可怕了。然后安静了。然后我听到这一声巨大的——她像是在敲门，尽可能用力，对吧？然后我听到一个声音，是我爸爸。我爸爸说，开门，开门！我意识到敲门的人不是她。所以我开了门，我爸爸站在那里。他说，你听到有人在走廊里尖叫吗？

00:21:09.260 --> 00:21:25.310
<v Elna Baker>So then I tell him this whole story. I'm like, there was this woman. She was in our room. I tell him all the details, and he is furious. He was so mad. And then he told us to stay there, shut the door, don't open the door. And then he went downstairs and talked to the hotel about it.
<v Elna Baker>所以我告诉了他整个故事。我说，有一个女人。她在我们房间里。我告诉了他所有的细节，他很愤怒。他非常生气。然后他叫我们待在那里，关上门，不要开门。然后他下楼和酒店谈了这件事。

00:21:25.310 --> 00:21:26.960
<v Ira Glass>Yeah, and what did he learn?
<v Ira Glass>是的，他了解到了什么？

00:21:26.960 --> 00:21:36.290
<v Elna Baker>They tell him that she had been outside. She was crying. They asked if she was a guest there. She said, yes. Her name was Elena Baker. And so they looked up the name.
<v Elna Baker>他们告诉他她一直在外面。她在哭。他们问她是不是那里的客人。她说，是的。她叫 Elena Baker。所以他们查了这个名字。

00:21:36.290 --> 00:21:37.190
<v Ira Glass>Elena Baker?
<v Ira Glass>Elena Baker？

00:21:37.190 --> 00:21:38.060
<v Elna Baker>Elena Baker, yeah.
<v Elna Baker>Elena Baker，是的。

00:21:38.060 --> 00:21:39.380
<v Ira Glass>And your name is Elna Baker.
<v Ira Glass>而你的名字是 Elna Baker。

00:21:39.380 --> 00:21:39.830
<v Elna Baker>Yes.
<v Elna Baker>是的。

00:21:39.830 --> 00:21:40.440
<v Ira Glass>OK.
<v Ira Glass>好的。

00:21:40.440 --> 00:21:44.960
<v Elna Baker>But I don't believe this part of the story. I think someone at the hotel just said this to him to cover.
<v Elna Baker>但我根本不相信故事的这部分。我认为酒店里的某个人只是为了掩盖事实才对他这么说的。

00:21:44.960 --> 00:21:46.760
<v Ira Glass>OK. And then they gave her a key?
<v Ira Glass>好的。然后他们给了她一把钥匙？

00:21:46.760 --> 00:22:00.860
<v Elna Baker>And then they gave her a key. Yep. And so the hotel offered him the whole stay for free, everything for free. And he's like, I don't care about your money. I want you guys to do a formal investigation as to how this happened.
<v Elna Baker>然后他们给了她一把钥匙。是的。所以酒店提出免费提供他整个住宿，一切免费。他说，我不关心你的钱。我要你们做一个正式的调查，关于这是怎么发生的。

00:22:00.860 --> 00:22:03.620
<v Ira Glass>Wow, that's such a white man takes charge move.
<v Ira Glass>哇，那真是一个典型的白人男性负责的行为。

00:22:03.620 --> 00:22:05.390
<v Elna Baker>My dad isn't white.
<v Elna Baker>我爸爸不是白人。

00:22:05.390 --> 00:22:06.850
<v Ira Glass>Oh, point taken.
<v Ira Glass>哦，明白了。

00:22:06.850 --> 00:22:08.700
<v Ira Glass>[LAUGHTER]
<v Ira Glass>[笑声]

00:22:08.700 --> 00:22:14.030
<v Elna Baker>He's a Hispanic man. Passionate, with such a Hispanic passion.
<v Elna Baker>他是西班牙裔。充满激情，带着一种西班牙裔的激情。

