<v Narrator>第 6 页。你不应该在这个页面上。从给你的选择中没有办法来到这里。你翻到这里是因为你厌倦了循环。你想出去。你比我聪明。翻到第 10 页。好吧。我去第 10 页。

00:34:38.790 --> 00:34:53.890
<v Narrator>Page 10. That night she fucks you as you lie there mutely, praying for it to be over, praying she won't notice you're gone. You have voided your body so many times by now that it is force of habit, reflexive as a sigh. It reminds you of-- [FADE OUT]
<v Narrator>第 10 页。那天晚上她强暴你，你哑口无言地躺在那里，祈祷它结束，祈祷她不会注意到你离开了。你已经多次清空了你的身体，以至于它成了习惯，像叹息一样反射性。它让我想起——[淡出]

00:34:53.890 --> 00:35:20.450
<v Narrator>[FADE IN] --sped up rotation of the Earth, the movement of the stars, the constellations melting into and out of being. You shudder and moan with precision. She turns off the lights. You watch the darkness until the darkness leaves you, or you leave it. To sleep, go to page 14. To dream about the past, go to page 11. To dream about the present, go to page 13.
<v Narrator>[淡入] ——地球的加速旋转，星星的运动，星座融入又消失。你精确地颤抖和呻吟。她关掉了灯。你看着黑暗，直到黑暗离开你，或者你离开它。睡觉，翻到第 14 页。梦到过去，翻到第 11 页。梦到当下，翻到第 13 页。

00:35:20.450 --> 00:35:29.020
<v Narrator>Page 13. You shouldn't be here, but it's OK. It's a dream. She can't find you here.
<v Narrator>第 13 页。你不应该在这里，但没关系。这是一个梦。她在这里找不到你。

00:35:29.020 --> 00:35:49.550
<v Narrator>In a minute, you're going to wake up, and everything is going to seem like it's the same, but it's not. There's a way out. Are you listening to me? You can't forget when you wake up. You can't go to page 14. I want to dream about the future. I go to page 12.
<v Narrator>一分钟后，你会醒来，一切看起来都一样，但并非如此。有出路。你在听我说话吗？醒来后你不能忘记。你不能翻到第 14 页。我想梦到未来。我去第 12 页。

00:35:49.550 --> 00:36:12.830
<v Narrator>Page 12. It's going to be all right. One day, your wife will gently adjust your arm if it touches her face at night, soothingly straightening it while kissing you. Sometimes, you will wake up just enough to notice. Other times, she'll only tell you in the morning. It's the kind of morning you could get used to. Go to page 14.
<v Narrator>第 12 页。一切都会好起来的。有一天，你的妻子会在晚上轻轻调整你的手臂，如果它碰到她的脸，舒缓地把它伸直，同时吻你。有时，你会醒来刚好注意到。其他时候，她只会在早上告诉你。这是你可以适应的那种早晨。翻到第 14 页。

00:36:12.830 --> 00:36:32.540
<v Narrator>Page 14. You wake up, and the air is milky and bright. The room glows with a kind of effervescent contentment, despite the boxes, and clothes, and dishes. You think to yourself, this is the kind of morning you could get used to. When you turn over, she is staring at you. The luminous innocence of the light curdles in your stomach. You don't remember ever going from awake to afraid so quickly.
<v Narrator>第 14 页。你醒了，空气奶白而明亮。尽管有盒子、衣服和盘子，房间闪烁着一种冒泡的满足感。你心想，这是你可以适应的那种早晨。当你转过身时，她正盯着你。光线的发光纯真在你的胃里凝固了。你不记得曾经这么快地从清醒变得恐惧。

00:36:32.540 --> 00:36:45.380
<v Narrator>"You were moving all night," she says. "Your arms and elbows touched me. You kept me awake." If you apologize profusely, go to page 2. If you tell her to wake you up next time your elbows touch her in your sleep, go to page 3.
<v Narrator>“你整晚都在动，”她说。“你的手臂和手肘碰到了我。你让我无法入睡。”如果你道歉，翻到第 2 页。如果你告诉她下次你的手肘在睡梦中碰到她时叫醒你，翻到第 3 页。

00:36:45.380 --> 00:37:14.340
<v Narrator>If you toss back the blankets from your body and hit the floor with both feet and tear through the house like it's Pamplona, and when you get to the driveway, your car keys are already in your hand, and you drive away with a theatrical squeal of the tires never to return again, go to page 15. Page 15. That's not how it happened, but OK. We can pretend. I'll give it to you, just this once. Turn to page 16.
<v Narrator>如果你从身上掀开毯子，两脚落地，像在潘普洛纳一样冲过房子，当你到达车道时，你的车钥匙已经在手里了，你以戏剧性的轮胎尖叫声开车离开，再也不回来，翻到第 15 页。第 15 页。这不是发生的情况，但好吧。我们可以假装。我给你一次机会，就这一次。翻到第 16 页。

00:37:14.340 --> 00:37:53.130
<v Narrator>Page 16, an end. In the pit of it, you fantasize about dying, tripping on a sidewalk and stumbling into the path of an oncoming car, a gas leak silently offing you in your sleep, a machete-wielding madman on public transit, falling down the stairs, but drunk, so you flop limb over limb like a marionette and feel no pain-- anything to make it stop. You have forgotten that leaving is an option.
<v Narrator>第 16 页，结束。在这一切的深处，你幻想死亡，在人行道上绊倒，跌入迎面而来的车道，煤气泄漏在睡梦中悄悄带走你，公共交通上的疯子挥舞砍刀，从楼梯上摔下来，但喝醉了，所以你像木偶一样肢体交叠，没有任何痛苦——任何能让它停止的事。你已经忘记离开是一个选项。

00:37:53.130 --> 00:38:34.270
<v Ira Glass>Zoë Winters, reading a chapter from Carmen Maria Machado's memoir In The Dream House.
<v Ira Glass>Zoë Winters，朗读 Carmen Maria Machado 的回忆录《梦之屋》中的一章。

00:38:34.270 --> 00:38:51.830
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, a family in quarantine thinks it's figured everything out, doing everything perfectly, until evidence of one tiny, tiny mistake. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，一个处于隔离状态的家庭认为已经弄清楚了一切，做得尽善尽美，直到出现了一个小小的错误。一分钟后，来自芝加哥公共广播，当我们的节目继续。

00:38:51.830 --> 00:39:12.150
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today's show, Stuck. For these long lockdown days, and weeks, and months, stories of people trapped in one situation or another for long stretches, stuck. I say this, recording these words in a closet at home. Here, I'm going to tap. That's the sound of me tapping hangers and a shirt.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》。我是 Ira Glass。今天的节目，困境。在这些漫长的封锁日子里，以及几周、几个月，关于人们在某种情况下被困在一段很长的时间里的故事，被困住了。我这么说，是在家里壁橱里录下这些话的。在这里，我要拍打。这是我拍打衣架和衬衫的声音。

00:39:12.150 --> 00:39:35.120
<v Ira Glass>So this writer named Damon Linker wrote this column for the magazine and website The Week about how, right now, with schools and jobs shut down, we've all become unmoored from the future. We're stuck in the present. And it's unclear when we're going to move forward to graduation, or a new job, or whatever else we're building for ourselves and our lives in a normal time we would be looking forward to.
<v Ira Glass>所以这个名叫 Damon Linker 的作家为杂志和网站《The Week》写了这篇专栏，关于现在，随着学校和工作关闭，我们所有人都从未来中脱离了。我们被困在当下。不清楚我们什么时候会向前迈进到毕业，或一份新工作，或任何我们在正常时间里会期待的为了自己和生活建立的东西。

00:39:35.120 --> 00:40:09.430
<v Ira Glass>And he tries to make the case that this is a big, big deal. "Human beings live their lives in time", he writes. "Our sense of ourselves is partly who we're trying to become." Quote, "A life without forward momentum is to a considerable extent a life without purpose, or at least the kind of purpose that lifts our spirits and enlivens our steps. Without the momentum and purpose, we flounder. A present without a future is a life that feels less worth living, because it's a life haunted by a shadow of futility." End quote.
<v Ira Glass>他试图证明这是一个非常、非常大的问题。“人类在时间中生活，”他写道。“我们对自己的一部分感官是我们试图成为的样子。”引述，“一个没有前进动力的生活在很大程度上是一个没有目的的生活，或者至少是那种能提升我们的精神、活跃我们的步伐的目的。没有动力和目的，我们就会挣扎。一个没有未来的现在是一种感觉不那么值得活下去的生活，因为它是一种被虚无的阴影所困扰的生活。”引用结束。

00:40:09.430 --> 00:40:38.740
<v Ira Glass>I have to say, I've thought about that so much these last few weeks, because so many people that I'm close to I just see how they are stressed out by how weirdly formless their days have become. The normal routine is all gone. And all of us have only the cloudiest sense of what the future is going to be. Act Three of our program today is about a family that is in that limbo, hunkered down, trying to get by. When you're stuck indoors for long stretches of time, weird things can happen.
<v Ira Glass>我必须说，过去几周我一直在思考这个问题，因为我和这么多亲近的人在一起，我看到他们是如何因为日子变得如此古怪地不成形而感到压力巨大。正常的作风全都消失了。我们所有人都对未来会是什么样子只有最模糊的认识。我们节目今天的第三幕是关于一个处于那种不确定状态的家庭，窝在家里，试图熬过去。当你被困在室内很长一段时间时，奇怪的事情可能会发生。

00:40:38.740 --> 00:40:43.230
<v Ira Glass>So let's get to this. This is Act Three, Cold Case.
<v Ira Glass>所以让我们进入这个。这是第三幕，冷案。

00:40:43.230 --> 00:40:58.530
<v Ira Glass>So you know those locked room detective stories where there's a crime in some room, and it's unclear how the culprit even got into the room? This next story is kind of about that sort of situation. One of our producers, Nadia Reiman, explains.
<v Ira Glass>所以你知道那些密室侦探故事，有些房间发生了犯罪，但不清楚罪犯是怎么进到房间里的？接下来的故事就是关于那种情况的。我们的制作人之一，Nadia Reiman，解释道。

00:40:58.530 --> 00:41:15.660
<v Nadia Reiman>This thing happened recently that made no sense to me at all. My family's been isolating in suburban Rochester, New York, where my parents live, and I mean hardcore isolating. We see no one, we go nowhere. We haven't been inside a store or been around other humans in two months.
<v Nadia Reiman>最近发生的一件事对我来说根本说不通。我的家人一直待在纽约州罗切斯特郊区自我隔离，我父母住那里，我的意思是彻底隔离。我们不见任何人，哪里都不去。我们两个月来没有进过商店，也没有接触过其他人。

00:41:15.660 --> 00:41:28.620
<v Nadia Reiman>For food, we do curbside pickup or delivery, which privilege, I know. Then we do this insane disinfection process where we wipe everything down with a 90% rubbing alcohol, 10% water solution we made ourselves.
<v Nadia Reiman>为了食物，我们做路边提货或送货，我知道这是特权。然后我们做了这种疯狂的消毒过程，我们用自己制作的 90% 的外用酒精和 10% 的水溶液擦拭一切。

00:41:28.620 --> 00:41:49.920
<v Nadia Reiman>When we're done, the room smells like a sterilized doctor's office. When we go for walks, if we see anyone, we cross the street a block ahead. We pick up all mail with disposable gloves, leave it in the garage for 24 hours. We used to spray with Lysol first. We agreed that was overkill. But to be honest, I still do it behind my family's back.
<v Nadia Reiman>当我们完成时，房间闻起来像消毒过的医生办公室。当我们散步时，如果我们看到任何人，我们提前一个街区过马路。我们用一次性手套处理所有邮件，在车库里放 24 小时。我们过去先用 Lysol 喷。我们同意那是过度了。但老实说，我仍然背着家人这样做。

00:41:49.920 --> 00:42:01.690
<v Nadia Reiman>So in my mind, there is no way any germ could break through our impenetrable disinfectant fortress. But somehow, after doing this day after day for seven straight weeks--
<v Nadia Reiman>所以在我看来，没有任何病菌能穿过我们刀枪不入的消毒堡垒。但不知何故，在连续七周每天这样做之后——

00:42:01.690 --> 00:42:07.640
<v Jeremy>Yeah, I woke up fairly early in the morning and felt it. I think I have a cold.
<v Jeremy>是的，我早上起得相当早，感觉到了。我想我得了感冒。

00:42:07.640 --> 00:42:09.190
<v Nadia Reiman>That's my husband, Jeremy.
<v Nadia Reiman>那是我的丈夫 Jeremy。

00:42:09.190 --> 00:42:15.080
<v Jeremy>I was rundown, runny nose, sore throat.
<v Jeremy>我感到精疲力竭、流鼻涕、喉咙痛。

00:42:15.080 --> 00:42:22.860
<v Nadia Reiman>In a couple of days, he was fine. But I was like, how the hell did that even happen? How could he possibly have gotten a cold?
<v Nadia Reiman>几天内，他好了。但我当时想，这到底是怎么发生的？他怎么可能感冒？

00:42:22.860 --> 00:42:44.970
<v Nadia Reiman>We've been super careful, because he has an immune deficiency, one that affects his respiratory system. It keeps me up at night, sometimes. Because if the cold virus made it into our house, then the coronavirus could too. It meant there was a crack in our armor. I needed to know where we had failed, so Jeremy and I consulted an expert at a very safe social distance.
<v Nadia Reiman>我们一直非常小心，因为他有免疫缺陷，一种影响他呼吸系统的缺陷。这有时让我彻夜难眠。因为如果感冒病毒进入了我们的房子，那么冠状病毒也可能进入。这意味着我们的铠甲上有一道裂缝。我需要知道我们在哪里失败了，所以 Jeremy 和我在一个非常安全的社交距离下咨询了一位专家。

00:42:44.970 --> 00:42:46.210
<v Nadia Reiman>Where are you?
<v Nadia Reiman>你在哪里？

00:42:46.210 --> 00:42:56.260
<v Ron Eccles>I'm in Cardiff, Wales, United Kingdom. And I'm on the coast here, so I'm looking out over the sea. So I'm really well isolated.
<v Ron Eccles>我在英国威尔士卡迪夫。我在海岸，所以我正看着大海。所以我隔离得非常好。

00:42:56.260 --> 00:43:08.400
<v Nadia Reiman>This is Ron Eccles, who really knows about colds. Studied them for 50 years. He ran a place called the Common Cold Center at Cardiff University. Almost immediately, I told him all the precautions we were taking.
<v Nadia Reiman>这是 Ron Eccles，他真的很了解感冒。研究了它们 50 年。他经营着卡迪夫大学的一个名为普通感冒中心的地方。几乎立刻，我告诉了他我们正在采取的所有预防措施。

00:43:08.400 --> 00:43:16.400
<v Nadia Reiman>I don't understand how this is physically possible. I just really want to know how did this happen? How could he have gotten this?
<v Nadia Reiman>我不明白这在物理上是怎么可能的。我只是真的很想知道这是怎么发生的？他怎么会得这个？

00:43:16.400 --> 00:43:34.320
<v Ron Eccles>Well, it's a bit of a mystery. Let me see if I can ask you some questions to work through it, rather like a detective. You say you've not actually met anybody, so I assume you've not physically touched anybody, shaken hands, or anything like that.
<v Ron Eccles>嗯，这有点神秘。让我试着问你一些问题来弄清楚它，就像侦探一样。你说你实际上没有见过任何人，所以我假设你没有身体接触过任何人，握手或类似的事情。

00:43:34.320 --> 00:43:36.340
<v Jeremy>No, not at all.
<v Jeremy>不，一点也没有。

00:43:36.340 --> 00:43:45.370
<v Ron Eccles>What about the door handle outside? Does anybody else touch that door handle, perhaps to open it? A cleaner or a delivery man?
<v Ron Eccles>外面的门把手怎么样？还有其他人接触那个门把手吗，也许是为了打开它？清洁工或送货员？

00:43:45.370 --> 00:44:00.050
<v Nadia Reiman>Well, so here's what I've done. Every time we go out the house, I started to spray the door handle with Lysol. And now I made the paint come off, so there's like a big streak going down the middle of it, because I've sprayed it so much.
<v Nadia Reiman>嗯，所以我做了这个。每次我们出门，我都开始用 Lysol 喷门把手。现在油漆都脱落了，中间有一条大条纹，因为我喷得太多了。

00:44:00.050 --> 00:44:04.290
<v Ira Glass>I can personally guarantee that doorknob is safe.
<v Ira Glass>我可以个人担保那个门把手是安全的。

00:44:04.290 --> 00:44:24.780
<v Ron Eccles>The next way you could pick up a cold from someone is by what we call an aerosol. And these are just tiny particles. Is there any way that someone could have been outside, and coughing and sneezing, and maybe small particles have come in? Is that a possibility?
<v Ron Eccles>从别人那里感冒的下一个方式是你所说的气溶胶。这些只是微小的颗粒。有没有可能有人在外面，咳嗽和打喷嚏，也许小颗粒进来了？那是可能性吗？

00:44:24.780 --> 00:44:38.710
<v Jeremy>That seemed like the only possible way. Because we will see people jogging, walking towards us, and we'll cross the street. And so if there's any potential for them to have sneezed or coughed in the trail--
<v Jeremy>那似乎是唯一可能的方式。因为我们会看到有人在跑步，走向我们，我们会穿过街道。所以如果他们有可能在路上打喷嚏或咳嗽——

00:44:38.710 --> 00:44:42.510
<v Ron Eccles>Yeah, that's a possibility, if you're caught downwind from them.
<v Ron Eccles>是的，如果你在顺风处被他们传染，那是有可能的。

00:44:42.510 --> 00:44:48.980
<v Nadia Reiman>I mean, sure, but pretty damn unlikely. Eccles moves onto a different line of questioning.
<v Nadia Reiman>我的意思是，确实，但非常不可能。Eccles 转向了另一条询问路线。

00:44:48.980 --> 00:45:10.140
<v Ron Eccles>I'm homing in now on two possibilities for Jeremy. Did Jeremy experience any itching at all with this episode that he had, itching in the throat or nose? Because if you had any itching, I would tend to think that it could be some sort of allergy.
<v Ron Eccles>我现在正在专注于 Jeremy 的两种可能性。Jeremy 在他经历的这一集中有任何发痒的感觉吗，喉咙或鼻子发痒？因为如果有任何发痒，我会倾向于认为这可能是某种过敏。

00:45:10.140 --> 00:45:12.850
<v Jeremy>No. No, not that I can recall.
<v Jeremy>没有。没有，我不记得了。

00:45:12.850 --> 00:45:27.190
<v Nadia Reiman>And he's been tested twice. No allergies. Which leaves one last possibility, one I didn't even imagine, like not in my wildest dreams. The thing that made him sick, it was inside him all along.
<v Nadia Reiman>而且他被测试了两次。没有过敏。这就剩下最后一种可能性，我甚至没想过，在最疯狂的梦中也没想过。让他生病的东西，一直都在他体内。

00:45:27.190 --> 00:45:59.660
<v Ron Eccles>We are carrying a lot of bacteria around in our throat and nose, normally. Now, if we get stressed-- and you did mention that Jeremy did have a bit of a slight immune deficiency, but stress causes us to get even more immune deficient-- then it's possible that some of the bacteria that are normally hanging around in the throat or nose of Jeremy have taken that opportunity of low immunity to make a bit of an infection.
<v Ron Eccles>我们通常在喉咙和鼻子中携带大量细菌。现在，如果我们感到压力——你确实提到 Jeremy 有一点轻微的免疫缺陷，但压力会导致我们更加免疫缺陷——那么，Jeremy 喉咙或鼻子中通常徘徊的一些细菌利用那个低免疫的机会制造了一点感染，这是有可能的。

00:45:59.660 --> 00:46:10.690
<v Ron Eccles>So I think I'm proving that it's probably a bacterial infection, because I can't see how on Earth you would have got a cold with all the precautions that you're taking.
<v Ron Eccles>所以我认为我正在证明这可能是细菌感染，因为我看不出你在采取所有这些预防措施的情况下怎么会感冒。

00:46:10.690 --> 00:46:13.780
<v Nadia Reiman>So he had a cold inside of him already?
<v Nadia Reiman>所以他体内已经有感冒了？

00:46:13.780 --> 00:46:19.660
<v Ron Eccles>Not a virus, but a bacterium. But it's masquerading as a common cold.
<v Ron Eccles>不是病毒，而是细菌。但它伪装成普通感冒。

00:46:19.660 --> 00:46:31.540
<v Nadia Reiman>That's his best guess. Basically, the call was coming from inside the house.
<v Nadia Reiman>那是他最好的猜测。基本上，电话来自房子里面。

00:46:31.540 --> 00:46:40.920
<v Nadia Reiman>How long would Jeremy have been like-- how long would it have been just hanging out there with him? Could it be like months? Could it be something that he had since before the seven weeks that we've been up here?
<v Nadia Reiman>Jeremy 会那样多久——它会和他待在一起多久？可能是几个月吗？可能是他在我们待在这里的七周之前就有的东西吗？

00:46:40.920 --> 00:46:43.090
<v Ron Eccles>Oh, yes. It could be years.
<v Ron Eccles>哦，是的。可能是几年。

00:46:43.090 --> 00:46:44.230
<v Nadia Reiman>Really?
<v Nadia Reiman>真的？

00:46:44.230 --> 00:46:45.850
<v Ron Eccles>Years.
<v Ron Eccles>几年。

00:46:45.850 --> 00:46:58.290
<v Nadia Reiman>It felt good. It meant there was no hole in the armor. All the wiping, the spraying, it was working. Everything was fine. Jeremy also felt good, kind of.
<v Nadia Reiman>感觉很好。这意味着铠甲上没有洞。所有的擦拭，喷洒，都有作用。一切都好。Jeremy 也感觉不错，某种程度上。

00:46:58.290 --> 00:47:09.300
<v Jeremy>I think in some ways, I might feel less relieved now, because now it feels like it just lives in me and that it's just always there waiting to try to kill me.
<v Jeremy>我认为在某些方面，我现在可能感觉没那么宽慰了，因为现在感觉它只是住在我体内，而且它一直都在那里等待着试图杀死我。

00:47:09.300 --> 00:47:14.020
<v Ron Eccles>Don't worry, Jeremy. We all have the same bacteria.
<v Ron Eccles>别担心，Jeremy。我们都有同样的细菌。

00:47:14.020 --> 00:47:15.870
<v Jeremy>But it's coming after me.
<v Jeremy>但它在追我。

00:47:15.870 --> 00:47:22.500
<v Jeremy>[LAUGHTER]
<v Jeremy>[笑声]

00:47:22.500 --> 00:47:52.380
<v Nadia Reiman>I have a small confession to make. When Jeremy first told me he felt sick, honestly, I didn't really believe him. I love him, but I thought he was just tired. Turns out, he was right. He was sick. It was real. So here you go. Jeremy, you were right. Sorry, love.
<v Nadia Reiman>我有一个小小的告白要说。当 Jeremy 第一次告诉我他感觉不舒服时，老实说，我真的不相信他。我爱他，但我以为他只是累了。事实证明，他是对的。他生病了。那是真的。所以给你。Jeremy，你是对的。对不起，亲爱的。

00:47:52.380 --> 00:48:19.480
<v Ira Glass>Nadia Reiman is one of the producers of our show.
<v Ira Glass>Nadia Reiman 是我们节目的制作人之一。

00:48:19.480 --> 00:48:37.010
<v Ira Glass>Act Four, Unhappy Accident. So we end our show today with a story from our archive of somebody stuck in a momentary difficult situation. It comes from Etkar Keret. The story is all true. It happens in Israel, where Etkar lives, and was read for us in English translation by actor, Michael Chernus.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕，不幸的事故。所以我们今天以来自我们档案的故事结束我们的节目，关于某人被困在暂时困难的情况下。它来自 Etkar Keret。这个故事完全是真的。它发生在以色列，Etkar 居住的地方，由演员 Michael Chernus 为我们翻译朗读。

00:48:37.010 --> 00:48:50.100
<v Narrator>"30 years I'm a cabbie", the small guy sitting behind the wheel tells me. "30 years and not one accident." It's been almost an hour since I got into his taxi in Beersheba, and he hasn't stopped talking for a second.
<v Narrator>“做了 30 年出租车司机，”坐在方向盘后面的小个子告诉我。“30 年了，没出过一次事故。”自从我在贝尔谢巴上他的出租车以来，已经快一个小时了，他一秒钟也没停过说话。

00:48:50.100 --> 00:49:05.460
<v Narrator>Under different circumstances, I would tell him to shut up, but I don't have the energy for that today. Under different circumstances, I wouldn't shell out 350 shekels to take a taxi to Tel Aviv. I would take the train. But today I feel that I have to get home as early as I can.
<v Narrator>在不同的情况下，我会叫他闭嘴，但我今天没那个精力了。在不同的情况下，我不会花 350 谢克尔打车去特拉维夫。我会坐火车。但今天我觉得我必须尽早回家。

00:49:05.460 --> 00:49:21.510
<v Narrator>I spent last night at Ichilov Hospital with my wife. She had a miscarriage and was bleeding heavily. We thought it would be OK, till she passed out. It wasn't until we got to the emergency room that they told us that her life was in danger and gave her a blood transfusion.
<v Narrator>我昨晚在 Ichilov 医院陪我的妻子。她流产了，流血很严重。我们以为会没事的，直到她昏了过去。直到我们到了急诊室，他们才告诉我们她的生命有危险，并给她输了血。

00:49:21.510 --> 00:49:38.060
<v Narrator>Three days before that, my dad's doctors told me and my parents that the cancer at the base of his tongue, which had been in remission for four years, was back. And the tumor was at such an advanced stage that the only way to fight it was to remove his tongue and larynx.
<v Narrator>在那之前的三天，我父亲的医生告诉我和我的父母，他舌根的癌症，已经缓解了四年，又回来了。而且肿瘤处于如此晚期的阶段，唯一的对抗方法就是切除他的舌头和喉头。

00:49:38.060 --> 00:50:07.250
<v Narrator>The oncologist said she didn't recommend having the surgery, but my dad said that he was for it. The oncologist told him that the operation would leave him seriously handicapped, unable to speak or eat. "At my age," my dad said, "all I need are my heart and my eyes, so I can enjoy watching my grandchildren grow." When we left the room, the doctor whispered to me, "Try to talk to him." She clearly doesn't know my dad.
<v Narrator>肿瘤科医生说她不建议手术，但我父亲说他支持。肿瘤科医生告诉他手术会让他严重残疾，无法说话或进食。“在我这个年纪，”我父亲说，“我只需要我的心脏和眼睛，这样我就可以享受看着我的孙子们成长。”当我们离开房间时，医生对我耳语道，“试着和他谈谈。”她显然不了解我父亲。

00:50:07.250 --> 00:50:36.580
<v Narrator>The taxi driver repeats for the hundredth time that, in 30 years, he hasn't had a single accident. And that all of a sudden five days ago, his car kissed the bumper of the car in front of him, traveling at 20 kilometers per hour. When they stopped and checked, he saw that, except for a scratch on the left side of the bumper, the other car hadn't really been damaged at all. He offered the other driver 200 shekels on the spot, but the driver insisted that they exchange insurance information.
<v Narrator>出租车司机第一百次重复说，在 30 年里，他没出过一次事故。而且五天前，他的车突然亲了一下前面那辆车的保险杠，时速 20 公里。当他们停下来检查时，他看到，除了保险杠左侧的划痕，另一辆车根本没损坏。他当场给了另一位司机 200 谢克尔，但那位司机坚持要交换保险信息。

00:50:36.580 --> 00:50:55.930
<v Narrator>The next day, the driver, a Russian, asked him to come to a garage. And he and the owner, probably a friend of his, showed him a huge dent all the way on the other side of the car and said the damage was 2,000 shekels. The cab driver refused to pay. And now the other guy's insurance company was suing him.
<v Narrator>第二天，那位司机，一个俄罗斯人，让他去一个修理厂。他和老板，可能是他的朋友，给他看了车另一侧的一个巨大凹痕，并说损失是 2,000 谢克尔。出租车司机拒绝支付。现在那个人的保险公司正在起诉他。

00:50:55.930 --> 00:51:13.840
<v Narrator>"Don't worry, it'll be OK", I tell him, in the hope that my words will make him stop talking for a minute. "How will it be OK?" he complains. "They're going to screw me. Those bastards are going to squeeze the money out of me. You see how unfair it is? Five days I haven't slept. Do you get what I'm saying?"
<v Narrator>“别担心，会没事的，”我告诉他，希望我的话能让他停止说话片刻。“怎么会没事？”他抱怨道。“他们会敲诈我。那些混蛋会从我身上榨钱。你看到它有多不公平吗？五天没睡了。你明白我在说什么吗？”

00:51:13.840 --> 00:51:33.590
<v Narrator>"Stop thinking about it", I suggest. Try thinking about other things in your life, happy things." "I can't", the cab driver groans and grimaces. "I just can't." "Then stop talking to me about it", I say. "Keep on thinking and suffering, but just don't tell me about it anymore, OK?"
<v Narrator>“别再想它了，”我建议。“试着想想你生活中的其他事情，快乐的事情。”“我做不到，”出租车司机呻吟并做鬼脸。“我就是做不到。”“那别再跟我说这件事了，”我说。“继续想，继续受苦，但只是别再告诉我了，好吗？”

00:51:33.590 --> 00:51:50.410
<v Narrator>"It's not the money", the taxi driver continues, "believe me. Yesterday, I went with my son to the graves of the Tzaddikim. We bought blessings for 1,800 shekels, and I didn't mind paying. It's the injustice that gets me." "Shut up", I say, finally losing it. "Just shut up for a minute!"
<v Narrator>“这不是钱的事，”出租车司机继续说道，“相信我。昨天，我和我儿子去了圣人墓。我们买了价值 1,800 谢克尔的祝福，我不介意支付。是不公正让我抓狂。”“闭嘴，”我说，最终失控了。“只要闭嘴一分钟！”

00:51:50.410 --> 00:52:04.930
<v Narrator>"What are you yelling for?" the cab driver asked, insulted. "I'm an old man. It's not nice." "I'm yelling because my father is going to die if they don't cut his tongue out of his mouth", I continued to yell. "I'm yelling because my wife is in the hospital after a miscarriage."
<v Narrator>“你为什么大喊大叫？”出租车司机问，受到侮辱。“我是个老人。这不礼貌。”“我大喊大叫是因为如果他们不切掉他的舌头，我父亲就会死，”我继续吼道。“我大喊大叫是因为我的妻子在流产后住院了。”

00:52:04.930 --> 00:52:25.910
<v Narrator>The driver is silent for the first time since I got into his taxi. And now I'm suddenly the one who can't stop the stream of words. "Let's make a deal", I say. "Get me to an ATM, and I'll take out 2,000 shekels and give it to you. In exchange, it'll be your father who has to have his tongue removed and your wife who's lying in a hospital bed getting a blood transfusion after a miscarriage."
<v Narrator>司机在我上车后第一次沉默了。现在突然是我无法停止话语流。 “做个交易，”我说。“带我去自动取款机，我取 2,000 谢克尔给你。作为交换，必须切除舌头的是你父亲，躺在医院病床上在流产后接受输血的是你的妻子。”

00:52:25.910 --> 00:52:47.320
<v Narrator>The driver is still silent. And now so am I. I feel a little uncomfortable for having shouted at him, but not uncomfortable enough to apologize. To avoid his eyes, I look out the window. We missed the exit to Tel Aviv. I tell him that, politely. Or I shouted, angrily. I don't recall anymore.
<v Narrator>司机依然沉默。现在我也一样。因为对他大喊大叫，我觉得有点不舒服，但没到不舒服到要道歉的地步。为了避开他的眼睛，我看向窗外。我们错过了去特拉维夫的出口。我礼貌地告诉他。或者我愤怒地大喊。我不记得了。

00:52:47.320 --> 00:53:14.270
<v Narrator>He tells me not to worry. He doesn't really know the way, but in a minute he'll find out. A few seconds later, he parks in the right lane of the highway after managing to convince another driver to stop. He starts to get out of the taxi to ask for directions to Tel Aviv. "You'll kill us both", I tell him. "You can't stop here." "30 years I'm a cabbie", he tosses back at me as he gets out of the taxi. "30 years and not one accident."
<v Narrator>他告诉我别担心。他其实不知道路，但过一会儿他会找到的。几秒钟后，他设法说服另一位司机停车后，把车停在高速公路的右车道上。他开始下出租车问去特拉维夫的路。“你会害死我们俩的，”我告诉他。“你不能停在这儿。”“做了 30 年出租车司机，”他下车时抛给我。“30 年了，没出过一次事故。”

00:53:14.270 --> 00:53:30.230
<v Narrator>Alone in the cab, I can feel the tears rising. I don't want to cry. I don't want to feel sorry for myself. I want to be positive, like my dad. My wife is fine now, and we already have a wonderful son.
<v Narrator>独自在车里，我能感觉到眼泪在涌出。我不想哭。我不想为自己感到难过。我想像我父亲一样积极。我的妻子现在很好，我们已经有一个很棒的儿子。

00:53:30.230 --> 00:53:48.900
<v Narrator>My dad survived the Holocaust. That's not just the half full glass, it's an overflowing one. I don't want to cry, not in this taxi, not next to this driver that I yelled at. The tears are welling up and will soon begin to flow.
<v Narrator>我父亲在大屠杀中幸存下来。那不仅仅是半满的杯子，那是溢出的杯子。我不想哭，不能在这辆出租车里，不能在那个我对他大吼大叫的司机旁边。眼泪正在涌出，很快就会开始流下。

00:53:48.900 --> 00:54:18.960
<v Narrator>Suddenly, I hear a crashing boom and the sound of windows breaking. The world around me shatters. A silver car veers across the next lane, completely smashed. The taxi moves too, but not on the ground. It floats above it, towards the concrete wall on the side of the road. After it hits, there's another bang. Another car must have hit the taxi.
<v Narrator>突然，我听到一声撞击声和窗户破碎的声音。我周围的世界破碎了。一辆银色轿车转向穿过下一条车道，完全被砸碎了。出租车也动了，但不是在地上。它漂浮在它上面，向路边的混凝土墙。在它撞击之后，还有另一声巨响。另一辆车一定撞上了出租车。

00:54:18.960 --> 00:54:39.730
<v Narrator>A second before the ambulance drives away, they load the taxi driver into it. Deep in my heart, I was hoping they'd send us in separate ambulances, but it just wasn't my day. The driver, looking revitalized and happy, lights a cigarette. The paramedic wears a yarmulke and tells me I was very lucky. An accident like that with no deaths is a miracle.
<v Narrator>救护车开走前的一秒，他们把出租车司机装了进去。内心深处，我希望他们用不同的救护车送我们，但这根本不是我的一天。司机看起来容光焕发、快乐地抽着烟。护理人员戴着基帕，告诉我我很幸运。那样的事故没有死亡真是个奇迹。

00:54:39.730 --> 00:54:54.400
<v Narrator>"The minute you're discharged from the hospital", he says, "you should run to the nearest synagogue and give thanks for still being alive." My cell phone rings. It's my dad. He's only calling ask how my day at the university was and whether the little one is asleep yet.
<v Narrator>“你出院的那一分钟，”他说，“你应该跑去最近的犹太会堂感谢仍然活着。”我的手机响了。是我父亲。他打电话只是问我在大学的一天怎么样，小家伙睡了没有。

00:54:54.400 --> 00:55:16.900
<v Narrator>I tell him that the little one is sleeping, and my day at the university was great. And Shira, my wife, is fine, too. She just stepped into the shower. "What's that noise?" he asks. "An ambulance siren", I tell him and try to sound casual. "One just passed by in the street."
<v Narrator>我告诉他小家伙在睡觉，我在大学的一天很棒。Shira，我的妻子，也很好。她刚进淋浴间。“那是什么噪音？”他问。“救护车警报器，”我告诉他，并试图听起来随便。“有一个刚刚在街上经过。”

00:55:16.900 --> 00:55:45.010
<v Narrator>Once, five years ago when I was in Sicily with my wife and son, I called my dad to ask how he was. He said everything was fine. In the background, a voice on a loudspeaker was calling a Dr. Schulman to the operating room. "Where are you?" I asked. "In the supermarket", my dad said without a moment's hesitation. "They're announcing on the loudspeaker that someone lost her purse." He sounded so convincing when he said that, so confident and happy.
<v Narrator>有一次，五年前我和妻子儿子在西西里时，我打电话给父亲问他怎么样。他说一切都好。背景中，扩音器里的声音在召唤 Schulman 医生去手术室。“你在哪？”我问。“在超市，”我父亲不假思索地说。“他们在扩音器里宣布有人丢了钱包。”他说那话时听起来那么令人信服，那么自信和快乐。

00:55:45.010 --> 00:56:09.700
<v Narrator>"Why are you crying?" my dad asks now from the other side of the line. I force myself to smile, hoping he can sense it, too. "It's nothing", I say, as the ambulance stops next to the emergency ward and the paramedic slams the ambulance doors open. "Really, it's nothing."
<v Narrator>“你为什么哭？”我父亲现在在电话那边问。我强迫自己微笑，希望他也能感觉到。“没什么，”我说，当救护车停在急诊室旁边，护理人员猛地打开救护车门时。“真的，没什么。”

00:56:09.700 --> 00:56:50.370
<v Ira Glass>Michael Chernus, reading a story by Etkar Keret. Etkar's most recent collection of stories is called Fly Already. And just this week, the new TV series that he wrote and directed with his wife Shira Geffen, Middleman, premiered on television in France and Germany. I have seen this show. It is amazing. I hope it gets to the United States soon.
<v Ira Glass>Michael Chernus，朗读 Etkar Keret 的故事。Etkar 最近的短篇小说集名为《Fly Already》。就在本周，他和妻子 Shira Geffen 编剧和导演的新电视连续剧《Middleman》在法国和德国的电视上首映。我看过这个节目。它很棒。我希望它能尽快进入美国。

00:56:50.370 --> 00:57:09.550
<v Ira Glass>Our program was produced today by one of our managing editors, Sarah Abdurrahman and Aviva DeKornfeld. The people who put our show together today includes Bim Adewunmi, Elna Baker, Emanuele Berry, Susan Burton, Dana Chivvis, Sean Cole, Noor Gill, Damien Graef, Lina Misitzis, Stowe Nelson, Katherine Rae Mondo, Nadia Reiman, Robyn Semien, Alissa Shipp, Lilly Sullivan, Chris Swetala, Matt Tierney, and Julie Whitaker.
<v Ira Glass>我们的节目今天由我们的管理编辑之一 Sarah Abdurrahman 和 Aviva DeKornfeld 制作。今天制作我们节目的人包括 Bim Adewunmi、Elna Baker、Emanuele Berry、Susan Burton、Dana Chivvis、Sean Cole、Noor Gill、Damien Graef、Lina Misitzis、Stowe Nelson、Katherine Rae Mondo、Nadia Reiman、Robyn Semien、Alissa Shipp、Lilly Sullivan、Chris Swetala、Matt Tierney 和 Julie Whitaker。

00:57:09.550 --> 00:57:18.730
<v Ira Glass>Our other managing editor is Diane Wu. Our executive editor is David Kestenbaum. Special thanks today to Harrison Nesbitt, Alexi Horowitz-Ghazi, and Eileen Berry.
<v Ira Glass>我们的另一位管理编辑是 Diane Wu。我们的执行编辑是 David Kestenbaum。今天特别感谢 Harrison Nesbitt、Alexi Horowitz-Ghazi 和 Eileen Berry。

00:57:18.730 --> 00:57:34.160
<v Ira Glass>I first read about Damon Linker's column in The Week, by reading Andrew Sullivan's column in New York Magazine. Our website thisamericanlife.org where you can stream our archive of over 700 episodes for absolutely free during your lockdown or your commute to your essential job.
<v Ira Glass>我第一次读到 Damon Linker 在《The Week》上的专栏，是通过读 Andrew Sullivan 在《纽约杂志》上的专栏。我们的网站 thisamericanlife.org，在那里你可以在封锁期间或通勤到基本工作岗位时免费流媒体播放我们超过 700 集的档案。

00:57:34.160 --> 00:57:58.710
<v Ira Glass>This American Life is delivered to public radio stations by PRX, the Public Radio Exchange. Thanks, as always, to our program's co-founder, Mr. Torey Malatia. You know, before he began his distinguished career in public broadcasting, Torey worked in an ice cream truck. But, I don't know, man, there were certain basics of the job I think he never got used to. Every single stop, he would greet the customers crowding around the window exactly the same way.
<v Ira Glass>《美国生活》由 PRX（公共广播交易所）分发给公共广播电台。一如既往地感谢我们节目的联合创始人 Mr. Torey Malatia。你知道，在他开始公共广播事业之前，Torey 在一辆冰淇淋车上工作。但是，我不知道，伙计，我认为他从未习惯工作中的某些基本知识。每一次停下来，他都会向挤在窗边聚集的顾客打招呼，方式完全相同。

00:57:58.710 --> 00:58:03.040
<v Elna Baker>You're just children. I didn't expect you to be children.
<v Elna Baker>你们只是孩子。我没想到你们会是孩子。

00:58:03.040 --> 00:58:08.040
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass, back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>我是 Ira Glass，下周回来，带来更多《美国生活》的故事。
